      "\"Collapsing the Caltrain tear didn't stop the bleed,\" Fourfootyest muttered, the brass vocoder on his collar clicking low in his throat. \"It just forced the pressure into the secondary pipes. Look at 6th and Market.\"",
      "Sam stood up slowly and walked over to the bank of small black-and-white CRT monitors stacked on the desk.",
      "Monitor 4 showed a rain-slicked alley off 6th Street, just south of Market. Under the flickering orange glow of a sodium streetlight, a dozen figures stood in a loose semi-circle around a steaming manhole cover. They were motionless, facing inward like tuning forks arranged in a dish.",
      "Fourfootyest twisted a knob with his clawed paw, zooming the pinhole lens in on the nearest figure—a woman in an oversized Army field jacket.",
      "Even on the grainy, low-resolution monitor, Sam could see that the infection had progressed far past what she’d witnessed at the train station. Thin, shiny ribbons of dark brown magnetic tape were spooling slowly out of the woman’s tear ducts and nostrils, drifting in the night breeze and tangling with the ribbons coming from the man standing beside her, weaving the group together into a literal, physical web of living circuitry. Their eyes didn't have pupils anymore; the irises had turned the smooth, curved gray of unplugged cathode-ray tubes, reflecting small squares of flickering snow.",
      "\"They’re not just repeating the signal anymore,\" Orpheus said softly, leaning over the bench behind Sam. \"They’re braiding it. Amplifying the gain.\"",
      "On Monitor 4, the woman in the field jacket suddenly stopped swaying.",
      "The ribbons of magnetic tape hanging from her face pulled taut, vibrating like plucked guitar strings. Slowly, with a stiff, ratcheting motion of her neck, she turned her head ninety degrees away from the circle and looked straight up at the utility pole where Fourfootyest’s hidden camera was mounted.",
      "She couldn't possibly see the lens—it was the size of a shirt button buried inside a rusted junction box twenty feet in the air.",
      "Yet she stared directly into the glass, and the corners of her mouth pulled back in a wide, mechanical smile.",
      "Down in the bunker, the heavy speaker cabinet sitting next to the oscilloscopes popped to life on a dead channel.",
      "\"HE IS STILL AT THE DOOR,\" the speaker boomed—not in one voice, but in a chorus of fifty overlapping voices recorded at different speeds. \"WE SEE THE COPPER ROOM, LITTLE DOG. WE SEE THE GIRL WITH THE OPEN TRACK.\"",
      "On the screen, every single Carrier in the 6th Street alley turned their gray glass eyes up toward the camera at once. As they did, the glass screen of Monitor 4 inside the bunker bulged outward with a loud creak, hairline cracks spiderwebbing across the thick lead-glass tube as a tendril of black static pressed against the inside of the display.",
      "\"Back up!\" Fourfootyest snarled, leaping across the workbench and bringing a heavy pair of insulated bolt cutters down onto the thick black coaxial cable feeding the monitor.",
      "Sparks showered across the desk as the cable parted. Monitor 4 died with a sharp pop, shrinking to a single white dot in the center of the cracked glass before going dark.",
      "In the sudden silence of the bunker, the only sound was the hiss of the severed cable smoking on the wood and the rapid, shallow rhythm of Sam’s breathing."
