      "\"She’s right,\" Fourfootyest barked, his metal claws flying over the dials. \"The ambient frequency across the entire seven-by-seven mile grid is climbing exponentially. Once that tower fires at full wattage, copper mesh won't save us. Every human brain from Daly City to Oakland with an active electrical synapse is going to be wiped and re-recorded in a single sixty-hertz sweep.\"",
      "Orpheus slammed the lid of a heavy steel footlocker shut and began laying gear out across the blueprint table with methodical speed: two sets of heavy rubber lineman’s gloves rated for forty thousand volts, insulated bolt cutters with three-foot fiberglass handles, copper grounding spikes, and a heavy brass marine flare gun loaded with high-intensity magnesium cartridges.",
      "\"Why magnesium?\" Sam asked, rubbing her temples as the green phosphor lines pulsed brighter behind her eyes.",
      "\"Because the distortion feeds on cold cathode light and low-band radio waves,\" Orpheus said, shoving a box of flare shells into his coat pocket and handing a compact, insulated crowbar to Fourfootyest. \"A pure, full-spectrum thermal flash blinds the tear’s tracking for a few seconds. It’s like shining a spotlight onto exposed camera film.\"",
      "He walked over to the old 1970s architectural blueprints of Sutro Tower pinned to the wall and tapped a finger against the triangular base building nestled beneath the three massive steel legs of the tower.",
      "\"The main transmission vault at the base of the center truss takes the power from the city grid and routes it up the hollow steel legs to the antennas,\" Orpheus explained. \"If we can get inside the main amplifier building, climb the interior maintenance shaft to the primary junction vault, and sever the main copper busbars before the capacitors discharge, the broadcast dies in its throat.\"",
      "Fourfootyest hopped onto the table, slinging a custom-built frequency jammer—a heavy box of copper coils, heat sinks, and vacuum tubes—onto his tactical chest harness and buckling the leather straps tight around his ribs.",
      "\"And if we’re thirty seconds late,\" the little Lhasa Apso growled through his vocoder, checking the charge on his forearm arc-welder with a sharp snap of blue sparks, \"we get front-row seats to the end of the world from nine hundred feet up. Let’s roll.\""
