    "number": 22,
    "slug": "chapter-22",
    "title": "The Choir of Clement Street",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Looking at a map, driving from the Presidio to Lands End is less than three miles down Lincoln Boulevard.",
      "In a city whose reality had dropped to eight frames per second, those three miles might as well have been the surface of the moon.",
      "When Orpheus steered the Volvo out from beneath the cypress grove toward the southern exit of the Presidio at 25th Avenue, they found the coastal highway completely impassable. The asphalt of Lincoln Boulevard overlooking Baker Beach hadn't just cracked; an entire quarter-mile section of the cliffside road was suspended ten feet in the air in pieces, like exploded LEGO bricks frozen mid-landslide against a backdrop of purple-and-cyan sky.",
      "\"Coastal route is gone,\" Orpheus said, wrenching the wheel hard left and dropping down into the foggy residential grid of the Richmond District. \"The fault line’s acoustic pressure is liquefying the sandy bluffs along the water. We have to cut south to Clement or Geary and take the avenues straight out to the beach.\"",
      "\"Take Clement,\" Fourfootyest said, propping his compact frame up on the center console between the front seats and locking a heavy coil of copper grounding wire into the jaws of his good left claw. \"Geary’s too wide—it’s a main transmission corridor. If they’ve set up an acoustic array down there, our alternator will die in half a block.\"",
      "Orpheus turned west onto Clement Street at 14th Avenue.",
      "Normally, even in the early morning hours, Clement was alive with delivery trucks unloading crates of bok choy outside dim sum bakeries, steam rising from kitchen vents, and early commuters waiting for the 2-Clement bus.",
      "Today, the street was a cathedral of frozen wax and humming wire.",
      "Every neon sign in the bakery and restaurant windows—*XIAO LONG BAO, GREEN APPLE BOOKS, COFFEE & DONUTS*—had turned the exact same blinding cyan hue, buzzing like angry hornets. And lining both sidewalks from 14th Avenue all the way out toward the ocean at 32nd were thousands of neighborhood residents arranged with terrifying geometric precision.",
      "They stood in pairs facing each other across the narrow width of the street, spaced exactly six feet apart like railroad ties. Stretching from the open mouth of each person on the north sidewalk to the open mouth of their partner on the south sidewalk was a taut, vibrating ribbon of black quarter-inch magnetic tape, suspended four feet above the asphalt right across the path of the car.",
      "Thousands of horizontal black ribbons stretched across Clement Street for eighteen blocks, turning the entire avenue into the interior of a mile-long cassette deck.",
      "\"They’ve webbed the street,\" Orpheus muttered, easing his foot off the gas as the Volvo rolled to a stop twenty yards from the first pair of linked Carriers at 15th Avenue. \"Every one of those ribbons is carrying high-voltage synaptic current between pairs. If we drive through them, the static charge across the hood will weld the engine block solid by 18th Avenue.\"",
      "In the passenger seat, Sam stared through the windshield at the thousands of taut black ribbons humming in the morning fog. With her newly calibrated eyes, she could see the invisible standing waves of purple static rippling down the avenue from ribbon to ribbon like dominoes.",
      "\"They aren't just blocking the road,\" Sam said, her hand tightening around the ten-inch aluminum master reel in her lap. \"Look at the rhythm of the waves. They’re pulsing at sixty hertz, with a millisecond gap between every cycle where the phase inverts.\"",
      "Fourfootyest’s red camera eye whirred as he looked from the street to the dashboard. \"A sync gap. Like the blanking interval between frames on an old CRT television.\"",
      "\"Not a gap we can drive through,\" Orpheus said, gripping the wheel. \"An eighty-eight Volvo doesn't move at sixty cycles a second.\"",
      "\"No,\" Fourfootyest growled through his collar vocoder, a fierce, reckless grin baring his canine teeth as he reached down with his left claw and ripped the plastic cover off the fuse box beneath the steering column. \"But if I wire the exterior steel body panels of this brick directly to Sam’s 19.8-hertz master reel, and we charge the bumper with an inverted wave off the battery...\"",
      "Orpheus looked down at the little Lhasa Apso. \"You want to turn the station wagon into an erase head.\"",
      "\"Swedish steel, baby,\" Fourfootyest barked, using his teeth and his single good claw to splice a heavy gauge jumper wire from the main battery lead to the bare metal door frame, then handing the stripped copper end of the return wire to Sam. \"Sam, clamp that wire against the aluminum flange of your mother’s reel and press your burned palm to the center hub. Orpheus, when she gives the word, you floor this bucket to fifty miles an hour and don't touch the brakes until we smell saltwater. If we drop below forty, the harmonic collapses and we become part of the bakery display.\"",
      "Sam wrapped her right hand around the bare copper wire, pressed it hard against the aluminum reel in her lap, and slapped her scarred left palm onto the center hub.",
      "Instantly, a low, warm, golden hum radiated out from her chest, running through the copper wire into the steel chassis of the Volvo. Across the hood of the wagon, a shimmering V-shaped wake of amber phase-light formed in the fog, humming in exact opposition to the cyan glare of the street.",
      "\"I have the phase lock!\" Sam gasped as the current arched through her ribs. \"Go! *Now!*\"",
      "Orpheus slammed the gas pedal to the floor.",
      "The four-cylinder engine roared, the rear tires chirped against the wet pavement, and the old station wagon rocketed straight into the gauntlet of Clement Street.",
      "*THWIP-THWIP-THWIP-THWIP-THWIP.*",
      "As the amber-charged grille of the Volvo hit the first dozen magnetic ribbons at forty-five miles per hour, the black tapes didn't wrap around the windshield—they vaporized on contact into harmless clouds of rust-colored iron-oxide dust, broken cleanly by the inverted phase wave pouring off Sam’s hand.",
      "On both sidewalks, block after block of paired Carriers jolted backward as their synaptic links were severed without killing them—people collapsing onto the concrete on 16th, 19th, 22nd Avenue, gasping and blinking in confusion as the static cleared from their eyes for the first time in hours.",
      "\"Hold the frequency, Sam!\" Fourfootyest yelled over the screaming engine as the speedometer needle climbed past fifty-five, the wagon fishtailing slightly through the intersection at 25th Avenue while bursts of amber and cyan sparks showered off the roof rack like a subway train hitting a third rail. \"Ten more blocks to the cliffs!\"",
      "Inside the car, the heat pouring through the copper wire was blistering Sam’s right hand, the aluminum reel growing hot enough to scorch the denim on her thighs. Her teeth rattled in her jaw as the entire Choir of the Richmond District turned its collective gaze toward the speeding wagon, trying to crush their amber bubble with sheer acoustic weight.",
      "The side rearview mirrors shattered simultaneously at 30th Avenue. At 32nd, the windshield cracked from top to bottom in a jagged lightning bolt.",
      "And then the buildings fell away on both sides, the street curved sharply north along the edge of the continent, and the vast, roaring gray expanse of the Pacific Ocean opened up before them."
    ],
    "excerpt": "Looking at a map, driving from the Presidio to Lands End is less than three miles down Lincoln Boulevard.",
