    "paragraphs": [
      "Orpheus drifted the smoking Volvo across the empty concrete turnaround at Point Lobos Avenue, slamming to a halt right against the splintered wooden trailhead barrier overlooking the ruins of the Sutro Baths.",
      "Under the hood, the overloaded alternator let out a final, dying whine and seized in a cloud of white radiator steam.",
      "Sam released the copper wire and sagged against the passenger door, her chest heaving, both hands trembling as she clutched her mother’s master reel to her ribs.",
      "They stepped out of the car into the salt wind—and stopped dead at the edge of the cliff.",
      "Below them lay the concrete skeletons of the nineteenth-century bathhouse ruins, where the Pacific surf normally crashed into rectangular tidal pools.",
      "Except the water in the ruins wasn't moving.",
      "Every wave breaking against the rocks of Lands End was frozen in jagged, twelve-foot-tall sculptures of black, glassy water streaked with glowing cyan foam. And rising straight out of the main subterranean drainage tunnel at the center of the ruins—where the transatlantic and fault-line grounding cables met the sea—was a towering vortex of inverted black static that spun slowly upward into the low clouds, pulling pebbles, driftwood, and salt spray skyward in defiance of gravity.",
      "Marching down the steep gravel switchbacks of the coastal trail from the Cliff House and Balboa Street were hundreds of late-stage Carriers, their bodies already fusing together at the shoulders with ribbons of magnetic tape as they converged on the tunnel entrance to guard the vortex.",
      "\"Look at the masonry inside the lower seawall,\" Orpheus said, raising a pair of scratched field binoculars to his yellow shooter’s glasses. \"Right behind the old tidal intake gate. The surge from the fault line blew the 1970s concrete camouflage clean off the cliffside. There’s a Signal Corps blast hatch ten feet above the frozen waterline.\"",
      "Fourfootyest hopped onto the wooden railing beside them, his red optical lens clicking to maximum magnification as he scanned the swarm of Carriers descending the switchback trail.",
      "\"They’re sealing the intake tunnel with their own bodies,\" Fourfootyest rasped, his damaged vocoder popping with static. \"Five minutes from now, that hatch is going to be buried under three tons of fused meat and copper wire.\"",
      "He dropped down from the rail, reached into the back of the dead Volvo with his good left claw, and dragged out Orpheus’s last heavy marine battery, a coil of quarter-inch steel tow cable, and the remaining three magnesium flare cartridges.",
      "\"There’s only one path down that cliff fast enough to beat the swarm,\" Fourfootyest said, pointing his fused right club-paw toward a rusted 1950s park service zip-wire and erosion-control cable that ran straight from the concrete retaining pillar at the Point Lobos overlook down to the central seawall of the baths. \"You two ride the pulley down over their heads and get that master reel inside the intake hatch.\"",
      "Sam looked down at the little cybernetic Lhasa Apso. \"What about you? You can't hang onto a pulley with one working paw!\"",
      "\"I’m not coming down the wire, kid,\" Fourfootyest said, already dragging the heavy marine battery toward the steel radio-repeater pole standing at the highest point of the cliff overlook, fifty yards away from the trail. With his teeth and his left claw, he began wiring the remaining remains of his vacuum-tube chest harness directly into the pole’s lightning arrestor. \"If you two try to drop into that basin while all five hundred of those walking tape decks are looking at the hatch, they’ll pluck you out of the air like pigeons. Somebody’s gotta ring the dinner bell up here on the ridge and pull their tracking north.\"",
      "\"Fourfootyest, no,\" Sam said, dropping to her knees in the wet gravel in front of him. \"Your chest rig is already fried! If you dump a full marine battery through your spine port without a heat sink, you won't have a single circuit left!\"",
      "The scruffy little dog stopped wiring for a second. His single dark, organic right eye looked squarely into Sam’s amber-ringed eyes, and for the first time since they’d met in the Tenderloin basement, he reached out with his cold, articulated left steel paw and rested it gently against her cheek.",
      "\"Hey,\" his vocoder rumbled softly, the static clearing into a warm, gravelly purr. \"Don't go getting sentimental on me now, Samantha White. Who else in this town was ever gonna listen to a two-foot mutt with a camera in his skull? You go down there and get your mom off the hook.\"",
      "He turned to Orpheus, who was hooking an iron snatch-block pulley over the rusted erosion cable at the cliff’s edge.",
      "\"Take care of the antenna, Orpheus!\" Fourfootyest barked, turning and bounding on three limbs up the rocky knoll toward the repeater pole. \"And when you get the city back on the air, somebody better leave a bowl of the good bacon grease by the workbench!\""
    ],
